%[text] # comp.TappedHorn Class
%[text] **J.G. Vermond, JVsound**
%[text] Tapped horn: the front of the diaphragm taps a horn with a blind end through a front chamber, the rear taps the same horn further along.
%[text] **Namespace:** comp
%[text] **Superclasses:** [comp.Enclosure](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','enclosure','enclosuredoc.m')))
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] **Draft.** This page is a first design of the acoustical model; the class is not implemented yet. The `comp.TappedHorn` class will model a tapped horn: the front of the diaphragm of the driver in `Driver` works on a front chamber, which is connected to a horn at the front tap. The horn is closed at its throat: between the throat and the front tap it is a blind end. The rear of the diaphragm is connected to the same horn at the rear tap, further along, so that both sides of the diaphragm load one horn, which radiates at its mouth. There is no closed rear chamber. For an FEA model of such an enclosure, see [`comp.FeaEnclosure`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','feaenclosure','feaenclosuredoc.m'))).
%%
%[text] ### Acoustical model
%[text] The acoustical side is the two-port network $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $ with the 4-port $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $ of the enclosure and the 4-port $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $ of the radiation, on the front conductor (top), the ambient pressure (middle, the reference) and the rear conductor (bottom), as for [`comp.FrontLoadedHorn`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','frontloadedhorn','frontloadedhorndoc.m'))):
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:t4h2)
%[text] - $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $, front conductor: the compliance $ C\_a = \\frac{V\_f}{\\rho c^2} $ of the front chamber to the ambient pressure, connected to the horn at the front tap. The horn consists of three 2-ports between the front conductor and the reference: the blind end $ \\mathbf{T}\_{h1} $ from the closed throat to the front tap, drawn below the front conductor, with its port at the throat open, the middle part $ \\mathbf{T}\_{h2} $ from the front tap to the rear tap, and $ \\mathbf{T}\_{h3} $ from the rear tap to the mouth. Seen from the front tap, the blind end acts as the impedance $ Z\_{h1} $ to the ambient pressure.
%[text] - $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $, rear conductor: the acoustic mass $ M\_a = \\frac{M\_{mi}}{S\_d^2} $ of the air that moves with the rear of the diaphragm in series, then the connection to the rear tap between $ \\mathbf{T}\_{h2} $ and $ \\mathbf{T}\_{h3} $. Unlike the other enclosures, $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $ couples the rear conductor to the front conductor.
%[text] - $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $: the radiation impedance $ Z\_{rad} $ of the mouth on the front conductor; as a first model a rigid piston in an infinite baffle, `zarad` with the radius $ r\_m $ of the mouth. The rear conductor passes through. \
%[text] As for the front-loaded horn, the horn is a chain of short uniform tubes $ \\mathbf{T}\_i $ of length $ \\Delta x $, each with the area $ S\_i $ of the horn profile at its middle, so that any profile can be used:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\mathbf{T}\_i = \\left\[ \\matrix{\\cos k \\Delta x & j \\frac{\\rho c}{S\_i} \\sin k \\Delta x \\cr j \\frac{S\_i}{\\rho c} \\sin k \\Delta x & \\cos k \\Delta x} \\right\] $
%[text] The three parts hold the tubes from the throat ($ x = 0 $) to the front tap at $ L\_f $, from the front tap to the rear tap at $ L\_r $, and from the rear tap to the mouth. The blind end is closed at the throat, where the volume velocity is zero; with $ \\mathbf{T}\_{h1} = \\left\[ \\matrix{A\_{h1} & B\_{h1} \\cr C\_{h1} & D\_{h1}} \\right\] $ from the throat to the front tap, that gives its impedance at the front tap:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\mathbf{T}\_{h1} = \\prod\_{x\_i < L\_f} \\mathbf{T}\_i,\\quad Z\_{h1} = \\frac{D\_{h1}}{C\_{h1}},\\quad \\mathbf{T}\_{h2} = \\prod\_{L\_f < x\_i < L\_r} \\mathbf{T}\_i,\\quad \\mathbf{T}\_{h3} = \\prod\_{x\_i > L\_r} \\mathbf{T}\_i $
%[text] **Open points of the design:** the horn profile and how it is given (a flare law or a table of areas), the number of segments, the positions of the taps, the losses of the front chamber (leakage and absorption, as in the rear chamber of the front-loaded horn), the air load on the front of the diaphragm (the driver takes it out of $ M\_{md} $), the losses in the horn, which would be part of $ \\mathbf{T}\_i $, the sign of $ Z\_{h1} $, which follows from the direction of $ \\mathbf{T}\_{h1} $, and the sign convention of the rear flow at the rear tap, where the rear conductor meets the front conductor.
%%
%[text] ## Creation
%[text] Not implemented yet. The planned syntax is `obj = comp.TappedHorn`, which creates a tapped horn with default dimensions and a default driver.
%%
%[text] ## Properties
%[text] Planned properties (not implemented yet):
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[200,120,-1]}
%[text] | Property | Symbol | Description |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `FrontChamberVolume` | $ V\_f $ | Volume of the front chamber in m3 |
%[text] | `ThroatArea` | $ S\_t $ | Area of the horn at the throat in m2 |
%[text] | `MouthArea` | $ S\_m $ | Area of the horn at the mouth in m2 |
%[text] | `HornLength` | $ L\_h $ | Length of the horn in m |
%[text] | `FrontTapPosition` | $ L\_f $ | Distance from the throat to the front tap, the length of the blind end, in m |
%[text] | `RearTapPosition` | $ L\_r $ | Distance from the throat to the rear tap in m |
%[text] | `Driver` | | Driver that is mounted in the enclosure, inherited from `comp.Enclosure` |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## Methods
%[text] Planned: `tae` and `tarad` with the elements above; `zaFront`, `zaRear` and `micTransfer` are inherited from `comp.Enclosure`, like `zarad` and `struve`. See [`solve2PortNetwork`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','methods','solve2portnetworkdoc','solve2portnetworkdoc.m'))) for how the 4-ports are reduced.

